Chapter: LexStubs

 One stub, for one function. This chapter used to carry deck-record and
 deck-short-of as well, on the premise that "outside the kernel there are no
 phase decks". That premise expired with Update 54.

 deck-record AND deck-short-of ARE GONE FROM HERE, and Update 54 is why.
 The lex and parse bundles now carry the real Core/PhaseAllocator.codex, so
 defining either one here would define it twice.

 The stub was never wrong, and that is the trap worth recording. `deck-record`
 is the IDENTITY in PhaseAllocator too, byte for byte; the real behaviour is a
 CALL-SITE transform, `emit-deck-record-wrapper`, gated on
 `deck-record-intrinsic` -- and that gate asks which CHAPTER defines the name,
 so a bundle without the Phase Allocator chapter gets the identity by design.
 That was correct and free until U54's `tokenize-collect` began rewinding the
 bivy after every token through `lex-state-below`, at which point the identity
 throws away the token records `make-token` believed it had deck-recorded, and
 the rung page-faults reading them back.

 A cite would have forced the chapter in and nothing would have needed
 deciding. Lexer.codex has no cites at all.

 THE REAL DEFINITION LIVES IN Syntax/SyntaxNodes.codex AND THE LEX SLICE
 CANNOT REACH IT. Bundling that chapter was tried first and measured, because
 using real code beats writing a stub: it needs `map-list`, which needs
 `codex/foreword/core/ListUtils.codex`, and then it needs OverflowMode,
 DiagnosticBag, AnnotationNode and the three Prose node types -- six more
 chapters, at which point the lex bundle IS the parse bundle and the cheapest
 rung stops being the thin front slice it exists to be.

 WHAT THIS STUB IS NOT EXERCISED BY, said plainly. A lex subject is compiler
 source and produces no lexical errors, so `lex-errors-push` is never called
 and neither is this. MEASURED rather than assumed: the U54 desugar rung, which
 lexes AstNodes.codex through the same lexer, reports `lex-tokens 5727` and
 `lex-errors 0`. A WRONG stub therefore would not show up in these rungs, which
 is the honest risk and the reason the argument is written out. If a subject
 ever does produce a lexical error, this identity hands back a diagnostic built
 in the bivy that `lex-state-below` is about to rewind -- the same shape as the
 defect above, one function over. It should become the real thing then, not be
 argued about.

Section: Stubs
  copy-sx-diag : Integer, Diagnostic -> Diagnostic
  copy-sx-diag (b) (d) = d
